   I wonder what the readers of ssrel-l said about that post.
 
   *I* think it was an excellent text in the way it reflects thinking
of a remarkable group of people. - People who are interested in studying
the world, who read popular scientific literature, who aspire to
understand the Grand Picture and try to push the world forward through
passionate expression of their ideas... that don't make any sense.
  I am well familiar with the type.   If I see children with such
thoughts, I am happy for them.  Adults are a desperate case though.
Such a text coming from a person over 20 is a clear indicator that
its author will *never* make any sense.
  Maybe, such people can still be useful for something...  After all,
the general audience will never tell them from real thinkers; the
passionate fuzzy-headed dreamers can actually help humanize the
unattractively nerdish image of a Thinker in the view of the populace.
Not that it matters much though...
 
  I wouldn't waste much space here criticizing the post - the author
has every single argument wrong; people who don't see holes in the text 
themselves should better move to that 'ssrel' list.
   Just one thing: as you all must have noticed, in the 'game' of Life
- "the best illustration of recursion" (whose short explanation is, again,
both vague and wrong), the consecutive steps are *iterations*, not recursion.
 
   Somehow whenever I see a person claiming insufficiency of the scientific
method as a reason for their 'religious beliefs', the real reason seems to
be their desire to achieve an illusion of wisdom despite lack of basic
education.
   I would like some day to talk to an *intelligent* person sharing
these beliefs...
 
Sasha.

