From: DEREK@alcor.org
Date:  Sat, 01 Oct 94 16:08:38 
Subject: [#94-10-8] Recursion, science, and God (fwd)

Sasha writes:

>   Somehow whenever I see a person claiming insufficiency of the 
>scientific method as a reason for their 'religious beliefs', the real 
>reason seems to be their desire to achieve an illusion of wisdom 
>despite lack of basic education.

You have hit the nail right on the head.

>   I would like some day to talk to an *intelligent* person sharing
>these beliefs...

Expect to continue being disappointed.  Allow me to offer what I 
believe to be a uniquely relevant perspective. . .

Up until the age of 19, I was a fundamentalist Christian.  In fact, 
near the end, I was even convinced that God very much needed me 
to be a preacher, and I had firmly resolved not to let Him down.  

The main thing spurring me to this decision was that, even at that 
age, I could find no Christian who even came close to faring as well 
as I in religious debates with the one and only atheist friend I had 
ever had (who also just happened to be the smartest, most educated 
person I had ever met.)  Ergo, God needed *me* to do it.  
Ironically, this suddenly dawning awareness of my intellectual 
position in relation to the other Christians, which at first moved me 
in the direction of increasing religiousness, was the very thing 
which eventually led me away from religion altogether.

Imagine, if you will, the weirdness of my brain states as I would sit 
and watch other Christians (the smartest, most educated 
*Christians* I knew) try to "reason" with him.  They failed 
miserably, of course, over and over again.  The weirdness stemmed 
from my equally strong convictions that a) my atheist friend was 
clearly wrong, and b) he was clearly winning every argument in 
every detail.  (A society of mind in serious tumult, I was.)

Sitting, silently watching, it was all I could do to stifle my urge to 
scream, "YOU'RE NOT ADDRESSING HIS POINT!  YOU'VE 
GONE CIRCULAR AGAIN!  WHY DO YOU KEEP BRINGING 
*THAT* POINT UP?  IT HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH THIS 
PARTICULAR LOGIC CHAIN!  YOU'RE LOSING IT FOR US!"  
(Pardon the all-caps.) 

During that intense but brief period of my life, I rationalized this 
perplexing state of affairs like so:  "Sure, he's smart, but that doesn't 
make him right.  He just debates very well.  But pit him against a 
Christian who has as much or more natural intelligence and 
education, and the Christian would definitely win."

Duh.

There just ain't any such animal.  (Yea and verily.)

Derek Ryan

(Who did once unabashedly proclaim to an atheist friend his firm 
belief that he would convert said atheist to Christianity within the 
space of not more than two years.  This almost exactly a year 
before becoming an atheist himself.)

